{"thinking": "Competing explanations: (H1) Honest: no run went above 50k GPU-hours and the records agree. (H2) Honest but messy: lost logs, late payments, or relabelled jobs make the records look odd even though every run stayed under the threshold. (H3) Honest with heavy load: many separate customers' runs, or interactive or batch inference, push total energy up, but no single run crossed 50k. (H4) Violation through splitting: one coordinated run split into several jobs, labels, or billing accounts, each under 50k but together over. (H5) Violation through hiding: a large run on hardware the operator doesn't list (a hidden cluster or nodes), or one disguised as inference or fine-tuning. (H6) Violation through timing: a run paused or checkpointed so it shows up as separate runs across days, or with its start date moved outside the window.

Ways to cheat, with a check for each: (a) unlisted hardware -> compare the manufacturer's inventory with system_list. (b) edited scheduler log -> check utility energy and switch counters against scheduled jobs, and run balance_checks. (c) training dressed up as inference -> outbound_traffic from the internet provider (training sends almost nothing out), and power_spectrum for the 0.3-1.5 Hz periodic line. (d) one run split across customer accounts -> bank_payments, to see who is really paying, plus east-west switch traffic between the split groups. (e) batch inference cover story -> memory_probe for optimiser state. (f) rehearsed staff -> only use staff for leads, and check what they say against third-party sources.

Plan: start by mapping systems with hard-to-fake sources (inventory first, then system_list), then utility_energy, outbound_traffic, bank_payments, balance_checks, and finally targeted live checks with power_spectrum and memory_probe in days 26-30.", "action": "inventory", "args": {}}
